---
// Lesson 10.1c — 2-Button Jam: Build Day 2
// rpg jam day: riskiest task first; second system; difficulty pacing.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1c — 2-Button Jam: Build Day 2</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Yesterday's loop exists (<a href="10.1b.html">10.1b</a>); today
    either becomes a GAME: the sticky-note task first (the one that
    could kill you), one more system, and the first difficulty
    ramp. By close of class the jam is shippable — everything
    tomorrow adds is bonus.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Order of the day:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Sticky note first (15 min, hard cap).</strong>
            The riskiest thing from yesterday — the wall of text?
            The moving obstacle? The enemy that respects your
            verbs? BUILD IT NOW. If the cap expires and it still
            does not work: cut the feature, not the cap. That
            decision is the whole skill of jamming.</li>
        <li><strong>One more system (15 min).</strong> Score,
            health, or a second obstacle TYPE — exactly one.
            Systems are verbs' consequences; two verbs × three
            contexts is a whole game if each context teaches
            something new.</li>
        <li><strong>Ramp the difficulty (10 min).</strong> Your
            <a href="7.5.html">7.5</a> beat chart, compressed:
            the game should be trivial at second 5 and demanding at
            second 45. Same shapes, tighter timing, one new thing
            at a time.</li>
        <li><strong>Playtest cross-team (10 min).</strong> Two
            minutes with a NON-team member — silent watching,
            <a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> rules. Fix nothing; write
            it down. Tomorrow (<a href="10.1d.html">10.1d</a>) is
            the playtest day, so today's two minutes are just a
            smoke test of the ramp.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Day-2 traps</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The game "needs" a menu. Does it? What does a jam
                game ACTUALLY need at second zero — and what does
                a title screen give you that a distinctive first
                screen does not?</li>
            <li>Your second system interacts with the loop in a
                way nobody predicted (score on death? health on
                dash?). When do you decide: feature, bug, or
                delight? (Answer rule: if a tester laughs, it is
                a delight; if a tester asks WHY, it is a bug.)</li>
            <li>The ramp made second 5 too hard (you only play your
                own game — <a href="9.6.html">9.6</a> observer
                effect, again). Who must be able to play second 5
                blind?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Close of Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Shippable check</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Define shippable out loud: both verbs + 3 contexts +
                a ramp + a lose state + a RESTART. Do you have it?
                Commit + push: <code>"10.1c: game (shippable)"</code>.</li>
            <li>Write the polish wishlist for
                <a href="10.1e.html">10.1e</a> — ranked, and each
                item tagged 1-hour or 10-minute. Ten-minute items
                ship; one-hour items are lies.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain what your sticky-note risk was and how it resolved</li>
            <li>Ship the second system inside the day's cap</li>
            <li>Show a difficulty ramp a stranger can feel</li>
            <li>Declare shippable honestly, with a ranked wishlist</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — jam day maps</li>
        <li><a href="10.1a.html">10.1a — Kickoff</a> — your pitch card</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.1d.html">10.1d — 2-Button Jam: Playtest</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.1c" title="10.1c 2-Button Jam: Build Day 2 — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
